::CW_U06_L06_GP_Q01::An author presentation begins with a short story excerpt. Which opening best prepares the audience?{
~A. I am reading something I wrote, and that is all you need to know.#Incorrect. The audience needs title, genre, and a useful frame.
~B. This is long, so I will explain every event before reading.#Incorrect. Too much summary can overwhelm the presentation.
=C. My piece is a short story called The Last Bus. Listen for the short sentences because they show panic.#Correct. This gives title, genre, brief context, and a listening focus.
~D. I picked this because it is my favorite.#Incorrect. Personal preference alone does not prepare the audience.
}

::CW_U06_L06_GP_Q02::Which excerpt choice is strongest for an author presentation?{
=A. A short section that clearly shows a craft choice and can be understood with brief context.#Correct. A focused excerpt helps the audience notice the writer's craft.
~B. The longest section, even if it needs ten minutes of explanation.#Incorrect. Long excerpts can make the presentation unfocused.
~C. A random middle section with no context.#Incorrect. The audience needs a clear entry point.
~D. The first page only because first pages are always best.#Incorrect. The best excerpt depends on the presentation purpose.
}

::CW_U06_L06_GP_Q03::Which statement is craft reasoning rather than plot summary?{
~A. The character goes outside, then comes back inside.#Incorrect. This only tells what happens.
~B. The poem is about rain.#Incorrect. This summarizes topic but does not explain craft.
~C. The story has three characters.#Incorrect. This identifies content but not writer choice or effect.
=D. I used short fragments during the argument so the pacing feels tense and interrupted.#Correct. This names a craft choice and explains reader effect.
}

::CW_U06_L06_GP_Q04::A student plans to read an excerpt but has no listening focus. What should the student add?{
~A. A complete summary of every previous draft.#Incorrect. The audience needs focus, not a full drafting history.
=B. A brief statement telling the audience what craft choice to notice.#Correct. A listening focus guides the audience toward the presentation purpose.
~C. A warning that the presentation may be confusing.#Incorrect. Planning should reduce confusion.
~D. A list of unrelated favorite books.#Incorrect. This does not support the selected work.
}

::CW_U06_L06_GP_Q05::Which reflection best connects revision to growth?{
~A. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This does not explain the revision or growth.
~B. I like the final version better.#Incorrect. This is a preference, not a craft explanation.
=C. I replaced direct explanation with dialogue so readers can infer the character's fear.#Correct. This names the revision and explains the reader effect.
~D. I do not remember what changed.#Incorrect. A presentation should include clear process reflection.
}
